<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Rusellae</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 294 BCE (approx.) · capital Rusellae</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Etruscan hill city near modern Grosseto, ringed by massive walls, which overlooked the Lake Prile lagoon until Rome captured it in 294 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Rusellae, modern Roselle, occupied two hills above the coastal plain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ombrone">Ombrone</a>, then bordering the lagoon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-prile">Lake Prile</a>. Settled from the Villanovan period, it grew into a city in the 7th century BCE, when houses of unbaked brick were built, and in the 6th century it was enclosed by walls of huge polygonal blocks some 3 km long.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its prosperity came from farming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ombrone">Ombrone</a> plain, fishing and salt in the lagoon, and links with the mining districts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vetulonia">Vetulonia</a>, which it overtook in importance during the 6th century BCE. Excavation has revealed a forum area, houses and craft quarters, but little is known of its government beyond the general Etruscan pattern of aristocratic magistrates.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-postumius-megellus">Lucius Postumius Megellus</a> stormed Rusellae in 294 BCE, taking 2,000 prisoners. As a Roman ally it sent grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio</a>'s fleet in 205 BCE, and later became a Roman colony with a forum, baths and amphitheatre. It remained a bishopric until the see moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grosseto">Grosseto</a> in 1138.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/rusellae.jpg" alt="Rusellae" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: LepoRello · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roselle_Archaeological_Park_Central_area_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Y